          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An
            %em
              algebraic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
            is a concept in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+algebra"} universal algebra
            that describes a specific type of algebraic gadget, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} groups
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} rings
            %em model
            of the appropriate theory. Roughly speaking, an algebraic theory consists of a specification of operations and laws that these operations must satisfy.
          %h3#categorical_formulation Categorical formulation
          %p
            Traditionally, algebraic theories were described in terms of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logical syntax
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} first-order theories
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signatures"} signatures
            have only function symbols, no relation symbols, and all of whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            are
            = succeed "s" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                equational law
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/equational+law"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+quantifier"} universally quantified
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations"} equations
            between terms built out of these function symbols). Such descriptions may be viewed as
            %em presentations
            of a theory, analogous to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generators+and+relations"} generators and relations
            as presentations of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
          %p
            In his thesis,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"} Bill Lawvere
            undertook a more invariant description of (finitary) algebraic theories. Here
            %em all
            the definable operations of an algebraic theory, or rather their equivalence classes modulo the equational axioms imposed by the theory, are packaged together to form the morphisms of a category with finite products, called a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
          %p
            This article is about generalized Lawvere theories. The article
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            treats the traditional notion of finitary, single-sorted Lawvere theories, with worked examples. The core of the present article is a working out of the precise connection between infinitary (multi-sorted) Lawvere theories and monads.
          %h3#basic_intuitions Basic Intuitions
          %p
            Intuitively, a Lawvere theory is the “generic category of products equipped with an object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of given algebraic type
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+object"} group object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            inside, and nothing more;
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            can be considered “the generic group”. Every object in the Lawvere theory is a finite power
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^n
            of the generic object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^n \to x
            are nothing but the
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            In other words, if we abstract away from the usual set-theoretic semantics, and consider a model for the theory of groups to be
            %em any
            category with finite products together with a specified group object inside, then the Lawvere theory of groups becomes a universal model of the theory, and carries all the information of the theory but independent of a particular presentation. In this way, theories and models of a theory are placed on an equal footing. A model of a Lawvere theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in a category with products
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is nothing but (i.e., is equivalent to) a product-preserving functor
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            is sent to is the given model of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is the Lawvere theory of groups, then a product-preserving functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to Set
            is tantamount to an ordinary group.
          %p The actual categorical construction of a Lawvere theory is described very easily and elegantly: it is the category opposite to the category of (finitely generated) free algebras of the theory. The free algebra on one generator becomes the generic object.
          %p
            If theories and models are placed on an equal footing, then what feature sets “theories”
            %em per se
            apart? In some very abstract sense, any category with products
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            could be considered a theory, where the
            = succeed "-models" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are product-preserving functors
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to D
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            serves to keep track of arities of operations.
          %p
            The generic “category of arities”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is, in the finitary case, the category opposite to the category of finite sets (opposite because the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            appears contravariantly in powers
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            should come equipped with a product-preserving functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi FinSet
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^{-}: FinSet^{op} \to T
          %p
            that is essentially surjective (each object of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is isomorphic to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^n
            for some arity
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h3#extensions Extensions
          %h4#infinitary_operations Infinitary operations
          %p
            Lawvere’s program can be extended to cover many theories with infinitary operations as well. In the best-behaved case, one has algebraic theories involving only operations of arity bounded by some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal+number"} cardinal number
            — or, more precisely, belonging to some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arity+class"} arity class
            — and these can be understood category-theoretically with a suitable generalization of Lawvere theories. In this bounded case, the Lawvere theory can be described by a small category, and the category of models will be very well behaved, in particular it is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+category"} locally presentable category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel-Ulmer+duality"} Gabriel-Ulmer duality
          %p Lawvere’s program can to some degree be extended further: one can work with Lawvere theories which are locally small (not just small) categories. Here, the theory might not be bounded, but at least there is only a small set of operations of each arity. Examples of such large theories include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The theory of algebras with arbitrary sums (one model of which is
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [0,\infty]
            %li
              %p The theory of sup-lattices, in which there is one operation of each arity, and
            %li
              %p The theory of compact Hausdorff spaces, where the operations are parametrized by ultrafilters.
          %p
            These examples go outside the bounded (small theory) case. Locally small theories in this sense are co-extensive with the notion of monad (on
            = succeed "):" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            and the category of models, and algebras of the theory are equivalent to algebras of the monad.
          %p
            In the worst case, there are algebraic theories where the number of definable operations explodes, so that there may be a proper class of operations of some fixed arity. In this case there are no free algebras, and Lawvere’s reformulation no longer applies. An example is the theory of complete Boolean algebras. (Note: category theorists who define a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U: A \to Set
            over sets to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+category"} algebraic
            if it is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic+functor"} monadic
            would therefore not consider the variety of algebras in such cases to be “algebraic”).
          %p
            Further commentary on these aspects may be found in the dozen or so comments in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/04/report_on_88th_peripatetic_sem.html#c023188"} this thread
          %p
            In summary, then, here is the connection between the logical and categorial descriptions, based on
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Johnstone"} Johnstone
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is:
          %ul
            %li
              %em small algebraic
              if it is given by a (small) set of operation symbols and equations;
            %li
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+category"} algebraic
              if it is given by a monad on the category of (small) sets;
            %li
              %em large algebraic
              if it is given by a (possibly proper) class of operation symbols and equations.
          %p Then any small algebraic category is algebraic, and any algebraic category is large algebraic, but neither implication may be reversed.
          %h4#multisorted_operations Multi-sorted operations
          %p
            Lawvere theories can also be generalized to handle multi-sorted operations. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is a set of sorts, then multisorted operations are of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi S
                  %msup
                    %mi s
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mi s
                  %mo →
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{s \in S} s^{n_s} \to t
          %p
            so that arities are functors
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n: S \to Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is seen as a discrete category. Thus, an infinitary multi-sorted Lawvere theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            involves an essentially surjective product-preserving functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mi S
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Set^S)^{op} \to T
          %p and the development goes through very much as in the single-sorted case.
          %h4#generalized_algebraic_theories Generalized Algebraic Theories
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+algebraic+theory"} generalized algebraic theory
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p For the moment we discuss the single-sorted case. The many-sorted case should be a straightforward extension.
          %p
            For any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal"} cardinal
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [n]
            be a set of that cardinality (sometimes we just use
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          .un_def
            %h6#definition_2 Definition:
            %p
              A
              %strong Lawvere theory
              or
              %strong algebraic theory
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally small category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              with small products that is equipped with an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              such that the (unique-up-to-isomorphism) product-preserving functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: Set^{op} \to C: [1] \mapsto x
            %p is essentially surjective.
          %h3#variations Variations
          %p Algebraic theories can be extended or specialized in various directions. Here are a few variations on the theme.
          %h4#essentially_algebraic_theories Essentially algebraic theories
          %p
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/essentially+algebraic+theory"} Essentially algebraic theories
            allow for partially-defined operations. Just as finitary algebraic theories can be understood as Lawvere theories, which live in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doctrine"} doctrine
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitely+complete+category"} finitely complete categories
          %h4#multisorted_algebraic_theories Multisorted algebraic theories
          %p
            %em
              %span.newWikiWord
                Multi-sorted theories
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/multi-sorted+theory"} ?
            allow for more than one sort or type in the theory.
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            be a set whose elements are called
            = succeed "." do
              %em sorts
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ob
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: S \to Ob(Set/S)
          %p
            which sends
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \in S
            to the object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s: 1 \to S
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set/S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to S
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set/S
            may be thought of as a monomial term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mi s
                  %msubsup
                    %mi x
                    %mi s
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_s x_{s}^{U_s}
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x_s\}
            is a set of variables indexed by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Set/S)^{op}
          %p
            Thus, objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Set/S)^{op}
            are pairs
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n, x: n \to S)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is any set, and morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n, x) \to (m, y)
            are functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: [m] \to [n]
            such that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = x \circ f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_i = x_{f(i)}
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi m
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in [m]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Set/S)^{op}
            has small products. In fact, any object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n, x)
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Set/S)^{op}
            is a product of objects of the form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i(s)
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Set/S)^{op}
              is the free category with small products generated by the set
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              be a category with small products and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ob
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi: S \to Ob(C)
              be any function. Define a functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi: (Set/S)^{op} \to C
            %p
              so that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n, x: n \to S)
              is taken to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi n
                      %mi Φ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{i \in n} \Phi(x(i))
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
              is a product-preserving functor and is, up to unique isomorphism, the unique product-preserving functor that extends
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Φ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi
          .num_def
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong multi-sorted algebraic theory
              over the set of sorts
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              consists of a locally small category with small products,
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Phi: S \to C
              such that the product-preserving extension
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi: (Set/S)^{op} \to C
            %p
              is essentially surjective. An
              %strong operation of arity
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1, \ldots, x_n \to y
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is a morphism of the form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi Φ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(n, x) \to \Phi(y)
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              has small products, a
              %strong model
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              is a product-preserving functor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M: C \to D
              %strong homomorphism of models
              is simply a natural transformation between product-preserving functors.
          %p
            It violates the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principle+of+equivalence"} principle of equivalence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            is an isomorphism on objects, since we can define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C'
            to have the same objects as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set/S
            and define hom-sets by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C'(x, y) = C(\Pi(x), \Pi(y))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Set/S)^{op} \to C
            evidently factors as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi Π
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Set/S)^{op} \stackrel{\Pi}{\to} C' \to C
          %p
            where the second functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C' \to C
            is an equivalence, so we may as well work with the functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi: (Set/S)^{op} \to C'
          %h4#commutative_theories Commutative theories
          %p
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebraic+theory"} Commutative algebraic theories
            are (single-sorted) algebraic theories for which each operation is an algebra homomorphism. These form an important subclass. Their categories of models are
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom+sets"} hom sets
            have a natural model-structure (algebra-structure), and the enriched Hom-functor has a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
          %p
            The theory of
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is perhaps the most familiar example. The theory of complete lattices and supremum-preserving functions is an interesting non-finitary example.
          %h2#RelationToMonads Relation to monads
          %p
            We flesh out the relationships between algebraic theories and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitary+Lawvere+theory"} infinitary Lawvere theory
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitary+monads"} finitary monads
          %h3#the_monad_of_a_locally_small_lawvere_theory The monad of a locally small Lawvere theory
          %p
            Suppose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a (locally small, multi-sorted) Lawvere theory, so we have a product-preserving functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi: (Set/S)^{op} \to C
          %p
            which we may assume to be the identity on objects. We define an adjoint pair between the category of models
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Mod
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod(C, Set)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to Set
            and transformations between them, and the category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set/S
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Prod
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prod(C, Set)
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Observe that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Set/S)^{op}
              is a Lawvere theory which is the theory of
              = succeed "-multi-sorted" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Prod
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo ≃
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ∘
                        %mi i
                    %msup
                      %mi Set
                      %mi S
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prod((Set/S)^{op}, Set) \stackrel{- \circ i}{\simeq} Set^S \simeq Set/S,
            %p
              where the first equivalence obtains precisely because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Set/S)^{op}
              is the free category with products generated by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Mod
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y: C^{op} \to Mod(C, Set)
            be the Yoneda embedding, taking
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            to the product-preserving functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom(c, -): C \to Set
          .un_thm
            %h6#theorem_1 Theorem 1
            %p The functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Π
                          %mi op
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi y
                    %mi Mod
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set/S \stackrel{\Pi^{op}}{\to} C^{op} \stackrel{y}{\to} Mod(C, Set)
            %p is left adjoint to the functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Prod
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi Prod
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Π
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Set
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Prod
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Prod(C, Set) \stackrel{Prod(\Pi, Set)}{\to} Prod((Set/S)^{op}, Set) \simeq Set/S
            %p (using the remark above).
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p We must exhibit a natural isomorphism
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Nat
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Π
                      %mi op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi x
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi x
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi G
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi Π
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Nat((y(\Pi^{op} (n \stackrel{x}{\to} S)), G) \cong Set/S(n \stackrel{x}{\to} S, G \circ \Pi \circ i)
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Nat
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Nat(-, -)
              indicates the hom-functor on the functor category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Mod
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod(C, Set)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi x
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G(\Pi(n \stackrel{x}{\to} S))
            %p
              by the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi x
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi G
                    %mi Π
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set/S(n \stackrel{x}{\to} S, G\Pi i)
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: S \to (Set/S)^{op}
              is the canonical embedding. Now both
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \circ \Pi
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi G
                    %mi Π
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set/S(-, G\Pi i)
              are product-preserving functors
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi S
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi op
                      %mo →
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Set/S)^{op} \to Set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Set/S)^{op}
              to check they give isomorphic results when restricted along
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mi Π
                    %mi i
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi G
                    %mi Π
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \Pi i \cong Set/S(i-, G\Pi i)
            %p
              However, because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: S \to (Set/S)^{op}
              is itself a Yoneda embedding
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi y
                        %mi op
                      %mo :
                      %mi S
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mi S
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y^{op}: S \to (Set^S)^{op}
          %p
            The
            %strong
              monad of a Lawvere theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is the monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T: Set/S \to Set/S
            associated with this adjunction.
          %h3#large_lawvere_theory_of_a_monad Large Lawvere theory of a monad
          %p
            Now let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T: Set/S \to Set/S
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set/S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u: 1 \to T
            and multiplication
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo :
                    %mi T
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m: T T \to T
          .un_def
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong large Lawvere theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Th
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Th(T)
              %strong
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite
              of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Kl
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi T
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi op
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Kl(T)^{op}
          %p
            The left adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi Kl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set/S \to Kl(T)
            is coproduct-preserving, so we have a product-preserving functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi S
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Kl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Set/S)^{op} \to Kl(T)^{op}
          %p
            which is a bijection on the classes of objects. Therefore
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Kl
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Kl(T)^{op}
            is indeed a (large, multi-sorted) Lawvere theory.
          .un_thm
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem 2
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              be a monad on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set/S
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Th
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Th(T)
              is isomorphic to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p In other words, we claim the monad of the adjunction
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi S
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Π
                          %mi op
                    %mi Kl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi y
                    %mi Prod
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Kl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
